From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pad8-arch-flatpak <noreply@example.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] namespace: do not treat a non-directory locked mountpoint
 as revealing

mount_too_revealing() refuses to mount a filesystem inside a user namespace
when the same filesystem is already visible in the parent namespace with
locked submounts that might hide content. The check walks the mounts of the
same filesystem and, for every MNT_LOCKED child, asks "is this directory
permanently empty?" via is_empty_dir_inode(). For a child whose mountpoint is a
regular file that test can never be true, so the whole filesystem is reported as
revealing and the mount fails with EPERM:

    bwrap: Can't mount proc on /proc: Operation not permitted

This port's /proc/cpuinfo is a bind mount of a regular file (the port rewrites
/proc/cpuinfo to hide SVE from llvmpipe), which trips exactly that path and makes
every Flatpak/bubblewrap sandbox unusable. A non-directory mountpoint cannot
hide directory content, so skip the emptiness test for it.

Signed-off-by: pad8-arch-flatpak <noreply@example.com>
---
 fs/namespace.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/fs/namespace.c b/fs/namespace.c
--- a/fs/namespace.c
+++ b/fs/namespace.c
@@ -4928,7 +4928,7 @@ static bool mnt_already_visible(struct mnt_namespace *ns,
 			if (!(child->mnt.mnt_flags & MNT_LOCKED))
 				continue;
 			/* Is the directory permanetly empty? */
-			if (!is_empty_dir_inode(inode))
+			if (d_is_dir(child->mnt_mountpoint) && !is_empty_dir_inode(inode))
 				goto next;
 		}
 		/* Preserve the locked attributes */
